From: Vaidehi312 <vaidehipandya13@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 00:17:04 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Overlap tile reads with the forward pass, and write latents
 in slices
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

real_encode_contrastive_from_checkpoint was spending most of its wall clock
outside the GPU. Two causes, both on the host side.

Reads were synchronous. The packaged .h5 stores one tile per gzip chunk, so
each batch read is `batches` separate decompressions on the calling thread,
and the GPU idled through every one of them. Batches are now produced by a
background thread through a depth-2 queue, so decoding the next batch overlaps
the current forward pass. One reader, not several: h5py serialises HDF5 calls
on a global lock, so extra threads do not decompress in parallel (measured: 8
threads bought 10%). Parallelising decode needs separate processes.

Latents were written a row at a time — two HDF5 calls per tile. Writing one
slice per batch measured ~37x faster on the same data. The metadata carry-
through loop had the same shape and got the same treatment.

The read also casts to float32 instead of letting `/255.` produce float64.
The placeholder is float32, so the wider intermediate was 4x the host memory
traffic for precision discarded on the next line. This is bit-identical: all
256 values a uint8 tile can hold round to the same float32 either way.

None of this changes an embedding. The projection graph is built with
is_train=False (BarlowTwins.py:217), so batch normalisation uses stored moving
averages rather than batch statistics, and real_images_2 bypasses augmentation
entirely — each tile's output is independent of what else is in its batch.
Equivalence is covered by backend/tests/test_encode_loop_equivalence.py in the
pipeline repo, over ragged tails, exact multiples and batch > dataset.

Two failure modes that were previously silent are now loud: a reader thread
that dies propagates its exception to the main thread instead of looking like
end-of-data, and a short read raises rather than leaving zero-filled rows in
an output that passes every completeness check downstream.

The near-identical encoders elsewhere in this file are untouched; this
pipeline does not call them.
